WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.297
"The Growth and Nutrient Intakes
of Children Raised on Plant-Based Diets"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.280
As I covered in my last video,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.760
the American Academy
of Nutrition and Dietetics

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.480
considers plant-based diets
suitable for pregnancy,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.600
as well as during lactation,
infancy, and childhood,

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.920
when they are well-planned.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.200
So, what goes into planning

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.960
a well-balanced
plant-based diet

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.280
for infants and kids?

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.480
For infants, breast milk
is the basis of nutrition,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.480
consisting not only
of critical nutrients

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.320
but also
immune-boosting compounds

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.760
providing both short
and long-term benefits.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.080
The World Health Organization
recommends breastfeeding

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.320
exclusively for the first
six months of life,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.840
and continuing
with the introduction

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.520
of complementary foods.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.200
What do we know about
the breast milk composition

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.280
of plant-based moms?

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.360
Vegetarian and vegan mothers
provide breast milk

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.120
with comparable
nutritional value,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.600
with a couple important caveats.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.240
In a direct comparison
of breast milk from omnivore

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.320
and vegetarian or vegan moms,

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.560
the notable difference
was lower DHA,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.560
the long chain omega-3,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.480
in the milk
from plant-based moms,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.680
due to their avoiding fish,
which is probably a good thing

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.960
given how contaminated
the aquatic food chain is.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.960
Thankfully,
ensuring proper supplementation

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.000
with pollutant-free sources
can bridge this gap,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.840
just like when there is
proper supplementation

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.680
with vitamin B12.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.440
So, provided that women
are eating enough

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.200
to cover their
nutritional requirements

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.880
and supplementing
with DHA and vitamin B12,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.200
vegan moms can produce
nutritionally similar milk.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.280
But what about
case reports like this

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.720
where an infant on a vegan diet
is failing to thrive?

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.080
Or this?

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.200
These case reports highlight
the importance of good nutrition

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.320
during this period of life
of extremely rapid growth.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.080
By around six months of age,

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.320
solid foods
should be introduced.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.560
By that age,
breast milk or formula alone

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.520
is not sufficient

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.960
to meet the nutritional
needs of infants.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.080
In the first case study,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.840
the infant
was exclusively breastfed

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.680
for 10 months,
and then was given

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.720
a nutritionally insufficient
rice-based drink.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.240
In the second case report,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.360
the infant did not receive
vitamin D supplementation,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.800
which is recommended for all
children in early life.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.560
Breast milk
should be continued on demand

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.360
until the child
is at least one year old.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.120
Formula is also an option
for women who cannot breastfeed.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.560
The VegPlate Junior,

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.800
a guide put together
to help with dietary planning

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.400
for plant-based kids,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.880
has a helpful visual,
and a more detailed table,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.960
for infants who have started
consuming complementary foods.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.600
And finally,
as we've highlighted before,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.240
raw diets are not appropriate
to meet the nutritional needs

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.560
of infants and children.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.160
So, what about nutrient status
and growth in vegan children?

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.760
First, caution must be applied

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.160
to some populations
defined as vegan,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.280
but who may follow diets
that refuse supplementation.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.400
This is where you can get the
impression from some experts

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.680
that vegan diets are
deficient and harmful.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.200
But the current
literature suggests

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.000
that plant-based diets
using supplementation

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.000
are likely to provide
the recommended amounts

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.400
of critical nutrients
to allow normal progression

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.240
of height and weight
in children,

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.400
and can be beneficial.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.320
There is a reason
to assume that vegans today

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.240
may be planning their diets
more adequately

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.920
than vegans in the past,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.600
as this way of eating
has been adopted

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.440
in the general population,

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.880
rather than existing
solely in fringe groups.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.840
And, lucky for us,
there is a new set of studies

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.800
aimed at tackling
this very question.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.200
What are the associations
between the modern practice

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.640
of vegetarian and vegan diets

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.920
in children and
adolescents and health?

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.560
Enter the VeChi study,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.360
the Vegetarian and Vegan
Children Study of German kids.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:42.880
For the one to three-year-olds

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.360
eating plant-based
in the VeChi study,

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.040
how did their growth compare
to their omnivorous peers?

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.160
Overall, vegan, vegetarian,
and omnivorous kids

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.240
did not significantly differ.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.160
This is in line
with other studies.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.520
One to three-year-old veg kids

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.280
tend to be slightly
smaller and lighter

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.960
(but within the normal range)

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.640
or have no differences
on average

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.320
compared to omni kids.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.200
When the researchers assessed
micronutrients and fatty acids,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.480
they noted special focus

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.480
should be paid
to critical nutrients

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.560
like vitamin D, iodine,
and DHA for all children,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.000
regardless of their diet,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.000
with extra attention
on riboflavin,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.000
vitamin B12, calcium,
and iron for veg kids.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.760
For a toddler, intake
of riboflavin (vitamin B2)

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.360
should be around
500 micrograms per day.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.600
The veg kids were short
by about 40-70 mcg.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.400
Luckily, there are
an abundant variety

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.640
of plant-based sources
like nuts, legumes,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.880
and fortified plant-
based milks.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.800
For example,
if your toddler drinks

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.560
a cup (240 ml)
of fortified soy milk,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.160
they have already met the
500 mcg recommended intake.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.800
For calcium,
great plant sources include

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:52.000
dark green vegetables,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.880
(like kale, bok choy,
and broccoli),

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.240
tofu, almonds,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.560
and calcium-fortified
non-dairy beverages,

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.400
like soy milk.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.920
What about the older
VeChi kids, aged 6 to 18?

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.720
Although iron intake was low
in the toddler group,

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.280
it was higher
in the older veg children,

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.000
surpassing the omnivore kids.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.320
There was no elevated risk for
iron deficiency in the veg kids.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.400
The higher intake of iron
in the veg group

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.760
may have helped compensate

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.280
for the lower bioavailability
of iron from plant foods.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:26.320
The kids in this group

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.960
were also more likely
to take supplements.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.720
Yet, in another recent study,

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.800
this one of 5 to 10
year old Polish kids

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.880
with adequate
reported iron intakes,

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.520
about 8 percent
had mild or moderate

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.160
hemoglobin deficiency.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.960
Regardless of diet,
about 3 percent

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.960
of one to two-year-olds
in the United States

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.200
have iron deficiency anemia,

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.760
and given its consequences
for young children,

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.920
prevention is a top priority.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.000
For adolescent
and young adult females,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.960
iron deficiency anemia
is around 6 percent,

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.280
and menstruation
increases this risk.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.800
It's worth
paying special attention

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.280
to include iron-rich foods
at most meals

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.760
and, to give a little boost
of iron absorption,

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.960
combine with
vitamin C rich foods.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.320
What else did we learn
about the older VeChi kids?

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.800
BMI did not differ
across groups.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.480
They were a pretty
healthy bunch.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:19.840
The plant-based kids had been
veg for four to five years

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.160
and most vegan kids
took supplements,

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.840
in particular B12,
as they absolutely should.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.240
Hopefully,
the 12 percent who weren't

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:31.600
were eating sufficient amounts
of B12-fortified foods,

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.760
as well as vitamin D.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.640
One way the groups did differ
was that the vegan kids

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.120
had significantly lower
LDL cholesterol

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.080
compared to the vegetarian
and omnivorous kids.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:43.920
And given that atherosclerosis,

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.720
our number one killer,
starts in childhood,

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.520
and dietary habits
track into adulthood,

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:50.880
plant-based diets in childhood

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.240
could ultimately improve the
cardiometabolic health of adults,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.640
as well as potentially
help prevent obesity,

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.073
which often persists
into adulthood.

